<title>NETHERLANDS: FORECAST-Dutch EMU deficit seen at 2.2 pct in 1997.</title>
<headline>FORECAST-Dutch EMU deficit seen at 2.2 pct in 1997.</headline>
<dateline>THE HAGUE 1996-09-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Finance Ministry published the following figures on Dutch central government finances for 1997 (billions of guilders unless otherwise stated):</p>
<p>							  1997    1996    1995</p>
<p>  Expenditure					203.1   201.9   231.8</p>
<p>  Revenues					   182.0   188.4   208.3</p>
<p>  Third parties account			    0.0     0.0     0.5</p>
<p>  Real budget deficit			     21.0    13.4    23.0</p>
<p>  Real budget deficit (as pct of GDP)	  3.0     2.0     3.6</p>
<p>  Official budget deficit			 19.8    18.6    22.1</p>
<p>  Official budget deficit (as pct of GDP)    2.8     2.8     3.5</p>
<p>  EMU deficit					 15.3    17.2    25.4</p>
<p>  EMU deficit (as pct of GDP)		    2.2     2.6     4.0</p>
<p>  EMU debt					   528.5   520.1   506.3</p>
<p>  EMU debt (as pct of GDP)			76.2    78.8    79.7</p>
<p>  Gross domestic product			 693.2   660.3   635.0</p>
<p>NOTES - The official budget deficit (beleidsrelevant tekort) excludes certain volatile items and is intended to portray a clearer picture of the underlying trend in government finances.</p>
<p>EMU deficit and EMU debt refer to the European Union's convergence criteria for economic and monetary union (EMU) and refer to entire public sector, not just to central government.</p>
<p>-- Amsterdam newsroom +31 20 504 5000, Fax +31 20 504 5040</p>
